WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.425 --> 00:00:10.534
SEBASTIEN MAROTTE:
[SPEAKING FRENCH]

00:10:57.530 --> 00:11:03.317
--and switch into English
to please welcome Ulku Rowe.

00:11:03.317 --> 00:11:04.150
Thank you very much.

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:18.580
ULKU ROWE: Good
morning, everyone.

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:24.360
It's great to be here in
Paris, City of Lights, la Ville

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:26.340
Lumieres.

00:11:26.340 --> 00:11:29.310
When I was getting ready
to come here from New York,

00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:32.370
I wondered how
Paris got that name.

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:34.560
So I Googled.

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:36.010
I found out two things.

00:11:36.010 --> 00:11:41.610
The first is did you
know that in 1860s there

00:11:41.610 --> 00:11:47.010
were 56,000 gas lamps
illuminating the streets

00:11:47.010 --> 00:11:49.820
and boulevards of Paris?

00:11:49.820 --> 00:11:53.600
The second why reason is
even more interesting.

00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:57.320
Paris got its name, the City
of Lights, because of the role

00:11:57.320 --> 00:12:00.890
that it played in
the 18th century

00:12:00.890 --> 00:12:04.020
in the Age of Enlightenment.

00:12:04.020 --> 00:12:08.340
So I can't think
of a better place

00:12:08.340 --> 00:12:12.750
to be talking about the
next set of revolutions that

00:12:12.750 --> 00:12:16.980
will bring in a different
age of enlightenment,

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:22.410
the age of innovation and
digital transformation.

00:12:22.410 --> 00:12:25.020
Now we're living
in exciting times.

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:27.900
It's a time of enormous change.

00:12:27.900 --> 00:12:31.770
We are living through a change
in our lives, in our society,

00:12:31.770 --> 00:12:34.470
and in business.

00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:38.280
And all of this is happening
at an unprecedented pace.

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:41.400
Now one of the places that
we can see this change

00:12:41.400 --> 00:12:43.510
is in the Fortune 500.

00:12:43.510 --> 00:12:47.940
The Fortune 500 was
first set up in 1955

00:12:47.940 --> 00:12:50.730
with an initial set
of 500 companies.

00:12:50.730 --> 00:12:56.990
Only 60 years later in 2014,
only 61 of those companies

00:12:56.990 --> 00:12:58.580
remain.

00:12:58.580 --> 00:13:01.700
Now if you are one
of the 439, that

00:13:01.700 --> 00:13:05.920
is a pretty scary statistic.

00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:10.600
And from that very
first 500 companies,

00:13:10.600 --> 00:13:12.985
nobody remembers, unless
you're a car enthusiast,

00:13:12.985 --> 00:13:16.240
the American car
maker Studebaker.

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:18.670
But we have welcomed Tesla.

00:13:18.670 --> 00:13:23.440
We don't remember Collins
Radio, but Netflix is here.

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:24.970
Companies are going
out of business.

00:13:24.970 --> 00:13:28.450
Companies are being acquired
and new companies are coming in.

00:13:28.450 --> 00:13:31.850
And it's happening faster
and faster every day.

00:13:31.850 --> 00:13:34.060
So what is driving
all this change?

00:13:34.060 --> 00:13:35.260
What's happening?

00:13:35.260 --> 00:13:39.220
What is the difference between
organizations that fail

00:13:39.220 --> 00:13:40.640
and those that thrive?

00:13:43.250 --> 00:13:45.930
And of course the
answer is technology.

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:47.990
Technology is everywhere.

00:13:47.990 --> 00:13:51.290
And this intersection of
various technology trends,

00:13:51.290 --> 00:13:55.010
cloud and mobile and
social and IoT and big

00:13:55.010 --> 00:13:58.490
data and machine
learning are having

00:13:58.490 --> 00:14:03.200
a profound impact in our
lives and our businesses.

00:14:03.200 --> 00:14:06.650
Now before I came to Google,
I had a pretty long career

00:14:06.650 --> 00:14:08.790
in financial services.

00:14:08.790 --> 00:14:12.800
And we had a we had a
very simple mission.

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:16.400
We were building
systems to enable

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:21.230
efficient, frictionless, safe
and sound financial markets.

00:14:21.230 --> 00:14:23.630
Unfortunately though,
doing that meant

00:14:23.630 --> 00:14:26.780
we had to spend an
inordinate amount of time

00:14:26.780 --> 00:14:30.020
on things like
hardware procurement

00:14:30.020 --> 00:14:32.810
and supply chain management
and network set up

00:14:32.810 --> 00:14:36.040
and databases and
software upgrades.

00:14:36.040 --> 00:14:40.070
Now cloud enables
you to change that.

00:14:40.070 --> 00:14:43.400
It enables you to
focus on technology

00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:46.040
that differentiates
your business,

00:14:46.040 --> 00:14:51.240
and de-focus technology
that does not.

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:53.840
And it doesn't end with cloud.

00:14:53.840 --> 00:14:56.900
With mobile and
social, businesses

00:14:56.900 --> 00:14:59.540
can reach their
customers, no matter where

00:14:59.540 --> 00:15:01.560
they are on the planet.

00:15:01.560 --> 00:15:05.270
And it's not just people,
devices as well with

00:15:05.270 --> 00:15:07.670
IoT, Internet of Things.

00:15:07.670 --> 00:15:10.190
And all these
interactions are now

00:15:10.190 --> 00:15:14.750
creating data that can be
processed with big data

00:15:14.750 --> 00:15:19.130
and machine learning to turn
into insights and engagement

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:21.500
models with our customers
that we were never

00:15:21.500 --> 00:15:22.490
able to do before.

00:15:25.550 --> 00:15:28.800
And this is available
to everyone,

00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:32.370
no matter how
large or how small.

00:15:32.370 --> 00:15:37.050
So it is no longer
about big versus small.

00:15:37.050 --> 00:15:41.940
Now it's about the
fast versus the slow.

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:44.250
Companies need to
be faster, they

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:46.620
need to be faster at
identifying customer trends.

00:15:46.620 --> 00:15:49.540
And they need to be faster
at responding to market.

00:15:49.540 --> 00:15:51.420
They need to be faster
at decision making.

00:15:51.420 --> 00:15:55.000
They need to be
faster at everything.

00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:57.710
Change is going to come.

00:15:57.710 --> 00:16:02.450
The question is are you
going to be an agent of it,

00:16:02.450 --> 00:16:03.980
or are you going
to be a casualty?

00:16:07.330 --> 00:16:09.870
Now the way to deal with
that changes of course

00:16:09.870 --> 00:16:11.700
again through technology.

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:15.720
At Google, we believe that every
company, regardless of its size

00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:20.250
or industry, will differentiate
themselves through technology.

00:16:20.250 --> 00:16:21.970
And all of that is
centered on data.

00:16:21.970 --> 00:16:26.790
So we believe that every company
is or will become a technology

00:16:26.790 --> 00:16:28.680
company.

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:31.560
And in this world,
your engineering talent

00:16:31.560 --> 00:16:35.280
is your only
sustainable advantage.

00:16:38.690 --> 00:16:41.540
So I've been a New
Yorker for 20 years,

00:16:41.540 --> 00:16:43.790
but I'm originally from Turkey.

00:16:43.790 --> 00:16:46.430
When I was growing up
in Turkey, the quality

00:16:46.430 --> 00:16:49.070
of your public transportation
kind of depended

00:16:49.070 --> 00:16:52.030
on your proximity to
your fellow passengers.

00:16:52.030 --> 00:16:55.880
So if you didn't have money,
buses were super cheap,

00:16:55.880 --> 00:16:58.906
but they were packed
like sardines.

00:16:58.906 --> 00:17:00.530
And if you had a
little bit more money,

00:17:00.530 --> 00:17:03.710
you could get a cab where
you could have the whole car

00:17:03.710 --> 00:17:07.910
to yourself, except now you are
at the mercy of your driver.

00:17:07.910 --> 00:17:10.819
And I am convinced that
every single driver in Turkey

00:17:10.819 --> 00:17:14.960
thinks they're a Formula One
driver, except the safety

00:17:14.960 --> 00:17:16.550
and the security.

00:17:16.550 --> 00:17:18.829
And in between
these two things we

00:17:18.829 --> 00:17:22.310
had what we called
the [TURKISH]..

00:17:22.310 --> 00:17:25.410
The literal translation of
that word means stuffed.

00:17:25.410 --> 00:17:26.510
It means stuffed.

00:17:26.510 --> 00:17:31.160
So these are smaller cars,
usually sedans or minivans.

00:17:31.160 --> 00:17:34.650
And they don't have fixed stops,
but they are fixed routes.

00:17:34.650 --> 00:17:37.880
And if you want to get on one,
you hail one and they stop.

00:17:37.880 --> 00:17:43.860
And if they have room to stuff
another person in, you get in.

00:17:43.860 --> 00:17:46.430
So because it's a
smaller car, they're

00:17:46.430 --> 00:17:49.210
limited by how many
people they can stuff

00:17:49.210 --> 00:17:51.030
by the number of their seats.

00:17:51.030 --> 00:17:55.440
So it's only marginally
more expensive than the bus,

00:17:55.440 --> 00:18:01.760
but the experience is
much better than the bus.

00:18:01.760 --> 00:18:08.780
So that was ride sharing
1.0 the Turkish way.

00:18:08.780 --> 00:18:14.000
Now today we're sharing, we're
experiencing ride sharing 2.0

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:15.890
the global away.

00:18:15.890 --> 00:18:17.900
Ride sharing companies
are completely

00:18:17.900 --> 00:18:23.000
transforming the transportation
industry all over the world.

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:27.110
They are using technology
to completely undermine

00:18:27.110 --> 00:18:29.360
the principles or
the assumptions

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:33.240
that the previous business
model was predicated upon.

00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:36.320
And it's not just the app that
allows you to hail the car

00:18:36.320 --> 00:18:39.350
and know where the car is
and the driver finds you,

00:18:39.350 --> 00:18:42.380
it's also things like
availability of the GPS

00:18:42.380 --> 00:18:45.920
and maps and voice, which means
that the driver doesn't have

00:18:45.920 --> 00:18:49.130
to memorize every road and
every point of interest

00:18:49.130 --> 00:18:51.050
that everybody
might want to know.

00:18:51.050 --> 00:18:53.480
And it's about being
able to give feedback,

00:18:53.480 --> 00:18:56.210
so that you can get a sense of
the credibility of the driver.

00:18:56.210 --> 00:18:58.790
So you're not at the
mercy of the Formula One

00:18:58.790 --> 00:19:00.980
drivers in Istanbul.

00:19:00.980 --> 00:19:04.100
And so this is all
has changed completely

00:19:04.100 --> 00:19:07.500
how transportation works.

00:19:07.500 --> 00:19:11.240
And it doesn't mean that Uber
or Lyft will be the winners.

00:19:11.240 --> 00:19:16.220
It just means that that industry
has been completely changed

00:19:16.220 --> 00:19:19.040
and it will never
be the same again.

00:19:19.040 --> 00:19:22.430
And most likely the next set
of disruption in that industry

00:19:22.430 --> 00:19:25.130
is going to be the self-driving
cars where you won't even

00:19:25.130 --> 00:19:28.390
need that cab driver.

00:19:28.390 --> 00:19:32.530
This is what we call
digital transformation,

00:19:32.530 --> 00:19:36.250
and it's not doing things
just a little bit better,

00:19:36.250 --> 00:19:39.740
it is doing things differently.

00:19:39.740 --> 00:19:43.720
It is about doing
different things.

00:19:43.720 --> 00:19:46.450
Now the question that you
should be asking here today

00:19:46.450 --> 00:19:50.210
is what does your digital
transformation look like?

00:19:50.210 --> 00:19:52.540
What does that
journey like for you?

00:19:52.540 --> 00:19:55.570
And that journey is
not to the cloud.

00:19:55.570 --> 00:19:57.790
Cloud is not a destination.

00:19:57.790 --> 00:20:03.800
Cloud is a vehicle that gets you
to your digital transformation.

00:20:03.800 --> 00:20:06.370
Now as we ask that
question, there

00:20:06.370 --> 00:20:09.230
are four things that
you need to think about.

00:20:09.230 --> 00:20:11.480
The first is optimization.

00:20:11.480 --> 00:20:13.840
How do you optimize
your IT operations?

00:20:13.840 --> 00:20:16.090
How do you get out
of the business

00:20:16.090 --> 00:20:19.180
of delivering technology
that doesn't differentiate

00:20:19.180 --> 00:20:21.630
your business?

00:20:21.630 --> 00:20:26.130
Now we say make it our
problem, because you know what?

00:20:26.130 --> 00:20:31.770
Delivering infrastructure and
platforms at extreme scale,

00:20:31.770 --> 00:20:34.500
with great reliability
and performance,

00:20:34.500 --> 00:20:40.110
and with trust and security is
what we do best here at Google.

00:20:40.110 --> 00:20:43.830
Second is collaboration.

00:20:43.830 --> 00:20:48.900
How do we help you create an
environment in your own shops

00:20:48.900 --> 00:20:50.940
that promotes innovation?

00:20:50.940 --> 00:20:52.860
And how do we create
an environment

00:20:52.860 --> 00:21:01.440
where the whole is greater
than the sum of the pieces?

00:21:01.440 --> 00:21:05.250
And it's not just about making
things a little bit faster

00:21:05.250 --> 00:21:09.310
and a little bit better, it's
about doing things differently.

00:21:09.310 --> 00:21:11.780
It's about doing
different things.

00:21:11.780 --> 00:21:13.710
Now we're going to talk
about acceleration.

00:21:13.710 --> 00:21:16.710
How can we help you
accelerate your business

00:21:16.710 --> 00:21:20.570
with data and machine learning?

00:21:20.570 --> 00:21:24.040
And finally, we're going
to talk about partnering,

00:21:24.040 --> 00:21:27.340
because that journey to
your digital transformation

00:21:27.340 --> 00:21:30.340
doesn't have to be one
that you walk alone.

00:21:30.340 --> 00:21:33.550
That partnership starts
with openness for us.

00:21:33.550 --> 00:21:36.070
And it's more than
just open source.

00:21:36.070 --> 00:21:39.130
It's about flexible
architectures and flexible--

00:21:39.130 --> 00:21:43.860
and customer friendly
costing structures.

00:21:43.860 --> 00:21:48.250
Now how can we take everything
that makes Google great

00:21:48.250 --> 00:21:49.630
and share that with you?

00:21:49.630 --> 00:21:50.560
And go beyond that?

00:21:50.560 --> 00:21:54.700
How do we co-invest in the
success of your business

00:21:54.700 --> 00:21:55.390
with you?

00:21:55.390 --> 00:21:58.210
We'll talk about that.

00:21:58.210 --> 00:22:01.930
And at Google, it is not
about just infrastructure,

00:22:01.930 --> 00:22:03.930
it's about the
collaboration platforms.

00:22:03.930 --> 00:22:06.540
It's about being able to
run your applications safe

00:22:06.540 --> 00:22:07.760
and securely.

00:22:07.760 --> 00:22:09.810
And it's about
data and analytics.

00:22:09.810 --> 00:22:12.210
And it's about machine learning.

00:22:12.210 --> 00:22:15.060
So during the course of
today's presentations,

00:22:15.060 --> 00:22:17.790
we are going to share with
you our thoughts on what

00:22:17.790 --> 00:22:20.550
that digital transformation
journey looks like,

00:22:20.550 --> 00:22:22.600
and the various
way stations in it.

00:22:22.600 --> 00:22:28.020
So let's start
with optimization.

00:22:28.020 --> 00:22:29.430
Well, congratulations.

00:22:29.430 --> 00:22:32.880
You have made the decision
to move to the cloud.

00:22:32.880 --> 00:22:36.660
You've made the decision
to adopt a technology

00:22:36.660 --> 00:22:40.620
platform that enables
you to focus on what's

00:22:40.620 --> 00:22:42.990
important for your
business to be more

00:22:42.990 --> 00:22:45.570
efficient, to
collaborate better,

00:22:45.570 --> 00:22:49.060
to innovate faster, be more
agile, be more responsive.

00:22:49.060 --> 00:22:52.530
Now how do you pick
your cloud provider?

00:22:52.530 --> 00:22:56.010
What factors help
you determine who

00:22:56.010 --> 00:23:00.750
is going to be the best partner
in that digital transformation?

00:23:00.750 --> 00:23:02.700
Now in my role, I
get to talk to a lot

00:23:02.700 --> 00:23:06.120
of C level executives and
IT professionals like you,

00:23:06.120 --> 00:23:08.930
and here is what they told me.

00:23:08.930 --> 00:23:10.980
Here's what you told me.

00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:13.680
You said it's about
reliability, it's

00:23:13.680 --> 00:23:17.910
about security, excellent
support, performance,

00:23:17.910 --> 00:23:22.180
and cost effectiveness
in that order.

00:23:22.180 --> 00:23:25.500
You picked these because
cloud infrastructure is not

00:23:25.500 --> 00:23:30.510
a commodity, because the
quality of the infrastructure

00:23:30.510 --> 00:23:34.440
that you are going to build
your business on matters.

00:23:34.440 --> 00:23:36.510
It makes a big difference.

00:23:36.510 --> 00:23:40.050
And these are the things that
actually differentiates us

00:23:40.050 --> 00:23:41.030
at Google.

00:23:41.030 --> 00:23:44.100
Our ability to run
at planet scale

00:23:44.100 --> 00:23:46.530
with great reliability
and performance,

00:23:46.530 --> 00:23:50.370
with trust and security,
with excellent partnership

00:23:50.370 --> 00:23:52.960
and good price.

00:23:52.960 --> 00:23:56.800
We have been building and
operating a planet scale

00:23:56.800 --> 00:23:58.720
cloud for a very long time.

00:23:58.720 --> 00:24:02.050
Now over the years, we
built seven platforms

00:24:02.050 --> 00:24:07.040
with over one
billion users each.

00:24:07.040 --> 00:24:10.370
Anyone recognize those logos?

00:24:10.370 --> 00:24:14.090
Raise your hand if you have used
any of these products for me?

00:24:14.090 --> 00:24:15.380
Raise your hand.

00:24:15.380 --> 00:24:16.760
Raise your hand.

00:24:16.760 --> 00:24:20.790
Keep your hand up if
you use them today.

00:24:20.790 --> 00:24:21.680
Yeah?

00:24:21.680 --> 00:24:23.870
And keep your hand
up if you've use them

00:24:23.870 --> 00:24:24.995
since I started talking.

00:24:28.900 --> 00:24:30.400
I appreciate the honesty.

00:24:30.400 --> 00:24:32.370
Thank you for being customers.

00:24:32.370 --> 00:24:36.250
But as we did that along the
way, we learned quite a bit

00:24:36.250 --> 00:24:38.680
about running a cloud at scale.

00:24:38.680 --> 00:24:42.460
And we learned about security
and reliability and efficiency,

00:24:42.460 --> 00:24:47.990
and all the experiences that
you enjoy about Google already.

00:24:47.990 --> 00:24:51.290
And it has that same
infrastructure and expertise

00:24:51.290 --> 00:24:54.320
that we offer to our
GCP clients so they

00:24:54.320 --> 00:24:56.970
can run their
businesses at scale

00:24:56.970 --> 00:24:59.990
with reliability and security.

00:24:59.990 --> 00:25:03.110
And now today, our
GCP clients are

00:25:03.110 --> 00:25:08.960
serving their over one
billion users on GCP.

00:25:08.960 --> 00:25:11.760
So how does Google
build reliable systems?

00:25:11.760 --> 00:25:12.560
How do we do it?

00:25:12.560 --> 00:25:13.980
It's not just one thing.

00:25:13.980 --> 00:25:15.290
It's many things.

00:25:15.290 --> 00:25:17.040
It's about how we
do data centers.

00:25:17.040 --> 00:25:18.890
It's how we think
about networking.

00:25:18.890 --> 00:25:22.700
It's about how we build
and deploy software.

00:25:22.700 --> 00:25:27.620
We build SRER Site Reliability
Engineering Practice years ago

00:25:27.620 --> 00:25:30.680
to be able to run ultra
scalable and reliable systems.

00:25:30.680 --> 00:25:35.000
And our engineers over time
have come up with new paradigms

00:25:35.000 --> 00:25:37.580
on how to run these
large systems.

00:25:37.580 --> 00:25:40.970
And they became with new
ways of introducing change

00:25:40.970 --> 00:25:45.200
at high velocity without
compromising from reliability

00:25:45.200 --> 00:25:47.000
or performance.

00:25:47.000 --> 00:25:50.050
So we literally did
write the book on it.

00:25:50.050 --> 00:25:53.810
We took all our experiences
and we put it together,

00:25:53.810 --> 00:25:55.670
and we published
it on our website.

00:25:55.670 --> 00:25:58.820
And it's free and
available for everyone.

00:25:58.820 --> 00:26:01.730
And it is that same
experience that we are now

00:26:01.730 --> 00:26:05.030
offering our customers with
our customer reliability

00:26:05.030 --> 00:26:07.670
engineering practice,
where we take

00:26:07.670 --> 00:26:10.870
our experiences, our
methods, and our frameworks,

00:26:10.870 --> 00:26:12.710
and now we share them with you.

00:26:15.380 --> 00:26:18.310
What's better than reliability?

00:26:18.310 --> 00:26:23.790
It's reliability that doesn't
come with a high price tag.

00:26:23.790 --> 00:26:27.570
Indeed, for dollar
for dollar, GCP

00:26:27.570 --> 00:26:30.150
provides better
performance value

00:26:30.150 --> 00:26:34.030
than any other cloud
provider in the market.

00:26:34.030 --> 00:26:36.750
This is from Cloud Spectator's
independent benchmarking

00:26:36.750 --> 00:26:38.910
study that was published
earlier this summer.

00:26:41.780 --> 00:26:44.880
And a key part of
delivering that performance

00:26:44.880 --> 00:26:48.600
is our global premium network.

00:26:48.600 --> 00:26:53.490
We run and operate the world's
largest IP data network.

00:26:53.490 --> 00:26:57.000
We have dark and light
fiber across every continent

00:26:57.000 --> 00:26:59.700
on the planet except
for Antarctica.

00:26:59.700 --> 00:27:03.300
We have an undersea
cables across the Atlantic

00:27:03.300 --> 00:27:05.160
and the Pacific.

00:27:05.160 --> 00:27:08.610
And our data centers don't
connect to just each other

00:27:08.610 --> 00:27:10.590
like some competing clouds do.

00:27:10.590 --> 00:27:17.030
Our data centers connects to
every single in the world.

00:27:17.030 --> 00:27:21.680
Today, about 40% of the
world's entire internet traffic

00:27:21.680 --> 00:27:24.740
goes through our network.

00:27:24.740 --> 00:27:27.890
We recently introduced a
premium network, in addition

00:27:27.890 --> 00:27:31.670
to our already super ultra
fast standard network.

00:27:31.670 --> 00:27:36.170
If you need it, it's
about 70% more performant.

00:27:36.170 --> 00:27:39.710
Now we pay incredible
amount of attention

00:27:39.710 --> 00:27:43.310
to the quality and the
reach of our network.

00:27:43.310 --> 00:27:45.030
Why do we do that?

00:27:45.030 --> 00:27:48.620
We do that because if you
are a business with customers

00:27:48.620 --> 00:27:51.470
all over the planet,
their performance

00:27:51.470 --> 00:27:53.660
and your reliability
of that network

00:27:53.660 --> 00:27:56.410
is going to be important to you.

00:27:56.410 --> 00:28:01.330
The other reason we do
this is for security.

00:28:01.330 --> 00:28:03.160
Remember we talked
about the five factors

00:28:03.160 --> 00:28:04.840
at the beginning
that was important?

00:28:04.840 --> 00:28:07.890
Security was number two.

00:28:07.890 --> 00:28:11.540
The reality is the
fewer public internet

00:28:11.540 --> 00:28:15.840
transit points you have in
your network, the less likely

00:28:15.840 --> 00:28:17.900
are you going to get cyber
attacks because you'll

00:28:17.900 --> 00:28:20.210
be eliminating things
like man in the middle,

00:28:20.210 --> 00:28:23.480
traffic profiling and
shaping, and surveillance.

00:28:23.480 --> 00:28:29.724
A network you control end
to end is inherently safer.

00:28:29.724 --> 00:28:34.190
At Google, our ability to
deliver security at scale

00:28:34.190 --> 00:28:36.530
is what differentiates us.

00:28:36.530 --> 00:28:39.290
Now it used to be that
security was the reason people

00:28:39.290 --> 00:28:41.120
didn't move to the cloud.

00:28:41.120 --> 00:28:47.120
Today, security is the
reason to move to the cloud.

00:28:47.120 --> 00:28:48.830
We believe in defense in depth.

00:28:48.830 --> 00:28:50.660
That means that
we embed security

00:28:50.660 --> 00:28:53.600
at every layer of our stack.

00:28:53.600 --> 00:28:55.910
We custom design and
build our servers

00:28:55.910 --> 00:28:57.470
and networking equipment.

00:28:57.470 --> 00:29:00.110
We eliminate any
unnecessary parts

00:29:00.110 --> 00:29:02.870
that would create
additional vulnerabilities.

00:29:02.870 --> 00:29:06.620
We've run proprietary and
hardened operating systems.

00:29:06.620 --> 00:29:09.440
And we run software stacks
that we constantly test

00:29:09.440 --> 00:29:12.000
and fix for vulnerabilities.

00:29:12.000 --> 00:29:15.230
Now above all of
this, we believe

00:29:15.230 --> 00:29:18.230
in a very simple philosophy.

00:29:18.230 --> 00:29:20.810
We trust nothing.

00:29:20.810 --> 00:29:25.760
We believe that if anything
can fail or can be compromised,

00:29:25.760 --> 00:29:29.330
it will fail and it
will get compromised.

00:29:29.330 --> 00:29:32.240
How far do we take
this philosophy?

00:29:32.240 --> 00:29:36.180
Let me introduce you to Titan.

00:29:36.180 --> 00:29:39.550
Titan is a custom chip
designed by Google.

00:29:39.550 --> 00:29:43.250
We put it on all our
news servers and hardware

00:29:43.250 --> 00:29:46.050
into network cards
in our devices.

00:29:46.050 --> 00:29:49.230
It allows us to protect the
identity of our machines

00:29:49.230 --> 00:29:51.690
by establishing a
hardware with a trust.

00:29:51.690 --> 00:29:54.060
It helps us authenticate
the hardware,

00:29:54.060 --> 00:29:57.510
and protects against
tampering at the hardware bios

00:29:57.510 --> 00:29:59.240
and services level.

00:29:59.240 --> 00:30:03.330
Now in Greek mythology,
titans were these big giants

00:30:03.330 --> 00:30:05.190
of incredible strength.

00:30:05.190 --> 00:30:08.970
Well, Titan packs all of that
in this tiny little chip.

00:30:08.970 --> 00:30:11.160
The Titan chip that
you see up there

00:30:11.160 --> 00:30:13.680
is smaller than my
fingernail on my pinky.

00:30:16.620 --> 00:30:19.750
Now if you are in
the audience today

00:30:19.750 --> 00:30:21.790
