00:41:12.260 --> 00:41:13.160
So I want to talk
about the Eiffel

00:41:13.160 --> 00:41:14.534
Tower, and the
collaboration that

00:41:14.534 --> 00:41:16.310
went into making the structure.

00:41:16.310 --> 00:41:18.560
I think a lot of people
believe, such as myself,

00:41:18.560 --> 00:41:20.106
I had thought that
Gustav Eiffel was

00:41:20.106 --> 00:41:21.980
a genius that designed
and created the Eiffel

00:41:21.980 --> 00:41:23.580
Tower himself.

00:41:23.580 --> 00:41:25.340
But when you study
the history, it's

00:41:25.340 --> 00:41:27.710
actually much more complex.

00:41:27.710 --> 00:41:30.410
The Eiffel Tower, the original
sketch that you see behind me

00:41:30.410 --> 00:41:33.589
was developed by two structural
engineers working together.

00:41:33.589 --> 00:41:35.630
In fact, this sketch was
developed by one of them

00:41:35.630 --> 00:41:37.054
while he was working at home.

00:41:37.054 --> 00:41:39.470
So next time your boss tells
you you can't work from home,

00:41:39.470 --> 00:41:42.770
just remind them that sometimes
your most creative elements

00:41:42.770 --> 00:41:46.110
come out when you're working
at home, away from the office.

00:41:46.110 --> 00:41:47.860
They brought the sketch
into their office,

00:41:47.860 --> 00:41:49.850
and they reviewed it
with a few other people,

00:41:49.850 --> 00:41:51.467
including their head
of architecture.

00:41:51.467 --> 00:41:53.050
The head of architecture
at their firm

00:41:53.050 --> 00:41:55.180
said this is a great
structure, but we

00:41:55.180 --> 00:41:57.470
need to make it much better.

00:41:57.470 --> 00:42:00.340
And so he added his insights.

00:42:00.340 --> 00:42:03.160
And as a result, they developed
some other embellishments

00:42:03.160 --> 00:42:04.600
such as the
decorative arches you

00:42:04.600 --> 00:42:08.860
see at the bottom the Eiffel
Tower, or the glass observatory

00:42:08.860 --> 00:42:11.720
that you see on the first floor.

00:42:11.720 --> 00:42:14.900
They then took this to Gustav
Eiffel who acquired the patent.

00:42:14.900 --> 00:42:18.170
And with a team of 300 people,
came up with the final designs

00:42:18.170 --> 00:42:21.560
and constructed the building
over the course of two years.

00:42:21.560 --> 00:42:23.960
The final achievement was the
tallest man made structure

00:42:23.960 --> 00:42:26.240
in the world at a time in 1889.

00:42:26.240 --> 00:42:29.750
And we'd certainly today
think of it as a masterpiece.

00:42:29.750 --> 00:42:31.949
Now although that
was 130 years ago,

00:42:31.949 --> 00:42:34.490
when you think about the world
of business today and the year

00:42:34.490 --> 00:42:39.530
2017, whether your business is
creating life saving medicines

00:42:39.530 --> 00:42:41.720
