00:44:59.700 --> 00:45:02.994
that final, final version
that they can present.

00:45:02.994 --> 00:45:04.410
This isn't a great
way of working,

00:45:04.410 --> 00:45:07.050
and it's not very efficient.

00:45:07.050 --> 00:45:09.660
When you look at how a G
Suite customer operates,

00:45:09.660 --> 00:45:11.520
collaboration is central.

00:45:11.520 --> 00:45:14.130
In Google Docs, one of our
most popular features today--

00:45:14.130 --> 00:45:16.050
and we launched
this 10 years ago--

00:45:16.050 --> 00:45:18.400
is the ability to
collaborate in real time.

00:45:18.400 --> 00:45:20.880
So you can have a set of
multiple editors and authors

00:45:20.880 --> 00:45:22.324
all working on a document--

00:45:22.324 --> 00:45:24.240
whether it's from their
laptop or whether it's

00:45:24.240 --> 00:45:26.310
from their mobile device--

00:45:26.310 --> 00:45:28.017
they can edit in real time.

00:45:28.017 --> 00:45:29.850
And they can see the
changes as they happen.

00:45:29.850 --> 00:45:31.900
If they have comments,
if they have questions,

00:45:31.900 --> 00:45:34.770
this can all be addressed
in real time making

00:45:34.770 --> 00:45:37.200
the process much, much faster.

00:45:37.200 --> 00:45:38.730
We pioneered this
and we continue

00:45:38.730 --> 00:45:42.880
to extend this collaboration
throughout our products.

00:45:42.880 --> 00:45:45.000
Another example is our
Team Drive feature which

00:45:45.000 --> 00:45:47.330
we launched earlier this year.

00:45:47.330 --> 00:45:50.720
So Drive is our cloud
based storage solution.

00:45:50.720 --> 00:45:52.460
And we create a
concept of a team.

00:45:52.460 --> 00:45:55.660
So now in Drive you can set up
a folder or a shared workspace

00:45:55.660 --> 00:45:57.970
for your team where you
can store all the files

00:45:57.970 --> 00:46:00.440
and information
related to that team.

00:46:00.440 --> 00:46:02.740
So now every member of
your team always has

00:46:02.740 --> 00:46:04.510
access to the latest files.

00:46:04.510 --> 00:46:07.060
They can collaborate in all
these files in real time.

00:46:07.060 --> 00:46:08.860
It doesn't matter
whether they're on the go

00:46:08.860 --> 00:46:10.234
or whether they're
in the office,

00:46:10.234 --> 00:46:12.202
they have access to this data.

00:46:12.202 --> 00:46:14.160
We've combined this with
our Enterprise Feature

00:46:14.160 --> 00:46:18.990
set such that Google Vault for
e-discovery and compliance.

00:46:18.990 --> 00:46:21.420
And there's some great
security features built in.

00:46:21.420 --> 00:46:23.700
Because the files
belong to the team,

00:46:23.700 --> 00:46:26.700
if a member leaves a team or
someone leaves the company,

00:46:26.700 --> 00:46:28.120
a team member
leaves the company,

00:46:28.120 --> 00:46:30.300
you can revoke access
to these files,

00:46:30.300 --> 00:46:32.594
but all the files stay
owned with the team.

00:46:32.594 --> 00:46:35.010
So the rest the employees that
are left behind in the team

00:46:35.010 --> 00:46:36.610
can continue working
at the same pace.

00:46:40.810 --> 00:46:42.670
Another example of
collaboration in G Suite

00:46:42.670 --> 00:46:45.360
is our Hangouts Meet video
conferencing solution.

00:46:45.360 --> 00:46:50.160
Video conferencing is integrated
seamlessly into G Suite.

00:46:50.160 --> 00:46:51.910
So it's very easy to
set up a meeting that

00:46:51.910 --> 00:46:53.990
has videoconferencing support.

00:46:53.990 --> 00:46:56.980
And whether an employee wants to
join from their mobile device,

00:46:56.980 --> 00:46:58.761
whether they want to
join from the laptop,

00:46:58.761 --> 00:47:00.760
or whether they're in a
meeting room with access

00:47:00.760 --> 00:47:04.090
to video conferencing
hardware, with one click

00:47:04.090 --> 00:47:06.340
they're seamlessly
out to the meeting.

00:47:06.340 --> 00:47:10.480
Now at Google we see a big trend
where the future of meetings

00:47:10.480 --> 00:47:13.776
is video conference moving
away from audio conference.

00:47:13.776 --> 00:47:15.400
And there's been some
academic research

00:47:15.400 --> 00:47:17.525
that's looked at audio
conferences in the workplace

00:47:17.525 --> 00:47:18.429
environment.

00:47:18.429 --> 00:47:20.470
And they found that people
who join in are simply

00:47:20.470 --> 00:47:23.080
participating via phone,
are a lot less engaged

00:47:23.080 --> 00:47:24.730
in the meeting,
they're much more

00:47:24.730 --> 00:47:27.610
likely to be more multitasking,
and they're a lot less

00:47:27.610 --> 00:47:29.590
likely to participate.

00:47:29.590 --> 00:47:32.470
With videoconferencing
that changes dramatically.

00:47:32.470 --> 00:47:33.970
People are more engaged.

00:47:33.970 --> 00:47:36.310
Humans have a natural
instinct to want to work face

00:47:36.310 --> 00:47:37.522
to face with individuals.

00:47:37.522 --> 00:47:38.980
So it doesn't matter
whether you're

00:47:38.980 --> 00:47:43.090
in different time zones
or different offices,

00:47:43.090 --> 00:47:46.010
you can seamlessly collaborate
through video conferencing.

00:47:48.650 --> 00:47:50.300
And that's collaboration.

00:47:50.300 --> 00:47:52.570
I now want to turn your
attention to machine learning

00:47:52.570 --> 00:47:54.530
and our visual intelligence,
and how that can

00:47:54.530 --> 00:47:56.540
impact the productivity suite.

00:47:56.540 --> 00:47:58.070
So at Google, we
have a huge focus

00:47:58.070 --> 00:48:02.090
on machine learning and AI, and
building not into our products.

00:48:02.090 --> 00:48:04.550
We want to create magical
product experiences

00:48:04.550 --> 00:48:06.170
throughout G Suite.

00:48:06.170 --> 00:48:09.200
Our focus is to automate
and let machines

00:48:09.200 --> 00:48:12.900
take care of the mundane
and the mindless tasks,

00:48:12.900 --> 00:48:15.860
and let humans really focus
on that creative work.

00:48:15.860 --> 00:48:17.900
So we want to automate
this 95% of stuff

00:48:17.900 --> 00:48:19.983
that gets in the way of
employees doing their best

00:48:19.983 --> 00:48:20.596
work.

00:48:20.596 --> 00:48:22.880
And I'm going to show you
some examples of how that

00:48:22.880 --> 00:48:24.600
works throughout the products.

00:48:24.600 --> 00:48:26.180
But Google is
uniquely positioned

00:48:26.180 --> 00:48:28.730
to focus on AI due to
our infrastructure, which

00:48:28.730 --> 00:48:30.590
you heard about earlier today.

00:48:30.590 --> 00:48:33.050
We have a world class
set of data scientists

00:48:33.050 --> 00:48:35.016
in machine learning
experts at Google.

00:48:35.016 --> 00:48:36.890
And finally, we have a
massive amount of data

00:48:36.890 --> 00:48:39.210
that we can use to
train our models.

00:48:39.210 --> 00:48:41.460
So let's see how machine
learning works in the product

00:48:41.460 --> 00:48:43.320
today.

00:48:43.320 --> 00:48:46.579
A simple example is
Drive Quick Access.

00:48:46.579 --> 00:48:48.120
So in our Drive
product, when someone

00:48:48.120 --> 00:48:50.880
comes in the product for
the first time or they're

00:48:50.880 --> 00:48:52.910
typically looking
to find a file.

00:48:52.910 --> 00:48:55.900
And it used to be you'd have
to search to find that file.

00:48:55.900 --> 00:48:58.500
Well, we created this feature
Quick Access, where now when

00:48:58.500 --> 00:49:02.310
you come into Drive, you'll see
a set of five suggested files.

00:49:02.310 --> 00:49:05.030
These are files we predict
you may want to open.

00:49:05.030 --> 00:49:07.170
And we look at a variety
of different signals.

00:49:07.170 --> 00:49:08.910
We look at the
time of day it is,

00:49:08.910 --> 00:49:10.450
we look at what
meeting you are in,

00:49:10.450 --> 00:49:11.990
based on what's
on your calendar,

00:49:11.990 --> 00:49:14.240
we look at who you've been
collaborating with recently

00:49:14.240 --> 00:49:16.080
on email or instant messaging.

00:49:16.080 --> 00:49:17.790
And using all these
various signals,

00:49:17.790 --> 00:49:21.480
our models will predict
which file you want to open.

00:49:21.480 --> 00:49:24.550
Now this saves users a lot of
time and it's very accurate.

00:49:24.550 --> 00:49:26.410
Over 40% of our
files stay in Drive

00:49:26.410 --> 00:49:28.300
are opened through this
quick access feature.

00:49:28.300 --> 00:49:30.299
And that number keeps
going up as our models get

00:49:30.299 --> 00:49:31.460
better and better.

00:49:31.460 --> 00:49:33.040
So a user on a
mobile device that's

00:49:33.040 --> 00:49:34.570
trying to find
that file quickly,

00:49:34.570 --> 00:49:37.150
can find it right away with
one click, start viewing it.

00:49:37.150 --> 00:49:37.940
Really powerful.

00:49:41.620 --> 00:49:43.770
Another example of machine
learning our products

00:49:43.770 --> 00:49:46.170
is the Gmail Smart
Reply features.

00:49:46.170 --> 00:49:47.940
Some of you may have
seen this before.

00:49:47.940 --> 00:49:50.490
But now in Gmail on
your mobile device,

00:49:50.490 --> 00:49:52.370
when you receive
an incoming email,

00:49:52.370 --> 00:49:54.360
we'll use artificial
intelligence

00:49:54.360 --> 00:49:56.840
to suggest three possible
responses for you

00:49:56.840 --> 00:49:58.680
for that email.

00:49:58.680 --> 00:50:01.520
Our studies show that a lot
of email communication today

00:50:01.520 --> 00:50:03.410
is short form in
nature and can be

00:50:03.410 --> 00:50:05.340
addressed with such a solution.

00:50:05.340 --> 00:50:08.150
So in this example you see
behind me, a colleague of mine

00:50:08.150 --> 00:50:11.810
has emailed me and asked me if
I've looked at the latest blog

00:50:11.810 --> 00:50:14.950
files to analyze them.

00:50:14.950 --> 00:50:17.540
With one click I can say,
yes, I'm working on it,

00:50:17.540 --> 00:50:19.015
and get back to them.

00:50:19.015 --> 00:50:20.640
Again, these are
small things, but they

00:50:20.640 --> 00:50:22.100
add up over the
course of the day

00:50:22.100 --> 00:50:24.570
and can save users a
lot of time, especially

00:50:24.570 --> 00:50:26.520
in that mobile use case,
when you're on the go

00:50:26.520 --> 00:50:29.599
and it's much more
difficult to type an answer.

00:50:29.599 --> 00:50:31.140
And this model gets
better and better

00:50:31.140 --> 00:50:35.894
as we have more data and
more usage of the product.

00:50:35.894 --> 00:50:38.210
The third example I
want to give today

00:50:38.210 --> 00:50:41.150
is for our Sheets product, which
is our cloud based spreadsheet

00:50:41.150 --> 00:50:42.500
solution.

00:50:42.500 --> 00:50:45.470
We launched a feature last
year called Explorer in Sheets.

00:50:45.470 --> 00:50:47.780
Now spreadsheets are
the most common way

00:50:47.780 --> 00:50:51.016
employees in your
enterprise will access data.

00:50:51.016 --> 00:50:53.390
And our studies have found
that a third of your employees

00:50:53.390 --> 00:50:55.265
aren't familiar with
formulas or don't really

00:50:55.265 --> 00:50:57.380
know how to use formulas
and spreadsheets.

00:50:57.380 --> 00:51:00.780
So it's very difficult for them
to get insights into the data.

00:51:00.780 --> 00:51:03.950
So with our Explore future
we made it easy for anyone

00:51:03.950 --> 00:51:06.997
to get data and insights
from their spreadsheet.

00:51:06.997 --> 00:51:09.080
And the way we do that is
through natural language

00:51:09.080 --> 00:51:11.090
processing, where
a user can simply

00:51:11.090 --> 00:51:14.270
ask questions of the data and
we'll show them the answers.

00:51:14.270 --> 00:51:15.830
So rather than
explain this, I think

00:51:15.830 --> 00:51:17.030
the best way is to see it.

00:51:17.030 --> 00:51:20.030
So I'd like to invite my
colleague Robert Kubis on stage

00:51:20.030 --> 00:51:21.864
to give you a demonstration
of this feature.

00:51:21.864 --> 00:51:22.363
Robert.

00:51:22.363 --> 00:51:23.734
ROBERT KUBIS: Thank you, David.

00:51:23.734 --> 00:51:26.602
[APPLAUSE]

00:51:28.514 --> 00:51:35.450
[FRENCH] so I will switch
to English as well.

00:51:35.450 --> 00:51:38.960
I assume everybody has seen
one of those, a spreadsheet

00:51:38.960 --> 00:51:41.290
in one form or the other.

00:51:41.290 --> 00:51:45.340
And tabular tables are
really, really powerful format

00:51:45.340 --> 00:51:48.260
to structure your data.

00:51:48.260 --> 00:51:50.250
So it sound maybe
a little scary,

00:51:50.250 --> 00:51:54.390
but it's no surprise to you
that most companies are actually

00:51:54.390 --> 00:51:57.290
relying on these kinds of data.

00:51:57.290 --> 00:52:00.590
But only, as David mentioned
already, only about 30%

00:52:00.590 --> 00:52:03.860
feel comfortable to get insights
out of their spreadsheet

00:52:03.860 --> 00:52:07.200
by using formulas, charts,
and things like that.

00:52:07.200 --> 00:52:11.210
So to enable more users to get
insights, actions, and charts

00:52:11.210 --> 00:52:15.670
out of their spreadsheets,
we added the Explore button

00:52:15.670 --> 00:52:16.820
to Sheets.

00:52:16.820 --> 00:52:20.330
So when I click here, some magic
has happened behind the scenes.

00:52:20.330 --> 00:52:22.400
And we give you some
actions and insights

00:52:22.400 --> 00:52:24.690
on the right side,
as you can see here.

00:52:24.690 --> 00:52:28.190
So we can see, for
instance for every increase

00:52:28.190 --> 00:52:34.020
of 100 euro in revenue, our
profit increases by 68.5 euros.

00:52:34.020 --> 00:52:36.110
So that's pretty nice.

00:52:36.110 --> 00:52:40.140
As you can see, this spreadsheet
is about dessert sales.

00:52:40.140 --> 00:52:42.260
So let's see if we
can find a nice chart.

00:52:42.260 --> 00:52:44.570
I think the pie chart
here is probably pretty

00:52:44.570 --> 00:52:47.420
appropriate for desserts.

00:52:47.420 --> 00:52:49.760
So what we can do is we
can just with one click,

00:52:49.760 --> 00:52:52.500
add the start into
our spreadsheet.

00:52:52.500 --> 00:52:54.080
Now imagine what
you would have to do

00:52:54.080 --> 00:52:57.890
if you would have to go to
the menu, select a chart,

00:52:57.890 --> 00:53:00.710
find the appropriate chart,
select the columns that you

00:53:00.710 --> 00:53:02.390
want to display in this chart.

00:53:02.390 --> 00:53:06.770
So you can see that this just
saved you a whole lot of time.

00:53:06.770 --> 00:53:10.700
Now if you don't find the
answer that you're looking for,

00:53:10.700 --> 00:53:13.280
you can actually use
natural language processing

00:53:13.280 --> 00:53:16.980
to see what data is
it in my spreadsheet.

00:53:16.980 --> 00:53:18.440
So what I want
you to see here is

00:53:18.440 --> 00:53:22.504
I want to look for the top
items sold in [INAUDIBLE]..

00:53:25.710 --> 00:53:29.100
And as you can see here,
we have some answers.

00:53:29.100 --> 00:53:32.130
It gives us also a formula.

00:53:32.130 --> 00:53:35.100
And I'm not the most
proficient in Sheets,

00:53:35.100 --> 00:53:37.980
but I probably could have
figured this formula out

00:53:37.980 --> 00:53:40.770
with my favorite search
engine, and looking

00:53:40.770 --> 00:53:44.940
how I can find and
do this calculation,

00:53:44.940 --> 00:53:47.370
but it would have taken
me quite some time again,

00:53:47.370 --> 00:53:50.020
and probably some
errors along the way.

00:53:50.020 --> 00:53:52.530
So here you just type
in your question,

00:53:52.530 --> 00:53:55.260
and you get the formula
presented to you

00:53:55.260 --> 00:53:56.850
and can use it.

00:53:56.850 --> 00:53:59.550
Now let's look at
something else.

00:53:59.550 --> 00:54:02.320
I want to see who are
the best sales reps.

00:54:02.320 --> 00:54:05.280
So we're also tracking
the sales rep here.

00:54:05.280 --> 00:54:08.750
So our sales rep [INAUDIBLE].

00:54:08.750 --> 00:54:10.510
And as you can see
here, Benjamin Button

00:54:10.510 --> 00:54:12.410
is the best sales
rep [INAUDIBLE]..

00:54:12.410 --> 00:54:16.250
And I can tweet this very
quickly and say in March

00:54:16.250 --> 00:54:20.670
and see Mary Tyler Moore is
coming up very, very quickly.

00:54:20.670 --> 00:54:22.820
So Benjamin should
watch his back.

00:54:22.820 --> 00:54:25.700
So with Explore
we added for users

00:54:25.700 --> 00:54:28.760
to get insights out of
their data very easily,

00:54:28.760 --> 00:54:32.450
and using natural language
processing and machine learning

00:54:32.450 --> 00:54:32.950
to do that.

00:54:32.950 --> 00:54:34.310
With that, thank you very much.

00:54:34.310 --> 00:54:35.780
Back to David.

00:54:35.780 --> 00:54:39.210
[APPLAUSE]

00:54:41.170 --> 00:54:44.180
DAVID THACKER: Thanks Robert.

00:54:44.180 --> 00:54:47.000
So that's one of the coolest
features in G Suite today.

00:54:47.000 --> 00:54:49.384
That's a great example of
artificial intelligence.

00:54:49.384 --> 00:54:51.800
And we're really just getting
started with these features.

00:54:51.800 --> 00:54:55.800
Again, we want to help companies
maximize their creative output.

00:54:55.800 --> 00:54:57.750
We want to democratize
access to data,

00:54:57.750 --> 00:54:59.750
and this is an important
stepping stone in that.

00:55:02.139 --> 00:55:04.680
So let's talk about customers
and how they're realizing value

00:55:04.680 --> 00:55:05.950
from this.

00:55:05.950 --> 00:55:09.780
So PWC, which is a large
global client services firm,

00:55:09.780 --> 00:55:12.480
migrated to G Suite over
the last couple of years.

00:55:12.480 --> 00:55:14.310
After they had
migrated to G Suite,

00:55:14.310 --> 00:55:16.200
they ran an internal
study to look at

00:55:16.200 --> 00:55:18.650
were their employees
actually saving more time.

00:55:18.650 --> 00:55:20.190
And what they found
in these results

00:55:20.190 --> 00:55:22.802
is that the average employee
was saving nine hours per week

00:55:22.802 --> 00:55:24.510
due to some of these
time saving features

00:55:24.510 --> 00:55:26.970
in G Suite and collaborative
nature of G Suite.

00:55:26.970 --> 00:55:28.800
That's a lot of time savings.

00:55:28.800 --> 00:55:30.960
When you think about nine
hours over the course

00:55:30.960 --> 00:55:34.300
of the entire employee
base, that adds up.

00:55:34.300 --> 00:55:36.300
That's more time to
focus on higher value

00:55:36.300 --> 00:55:39.090
activities such as serving
and advising clients.

00:55:39.090 --> 00:55:40.590
It's really a
powerful game changer.

00:55:43.310 --> 00:55:45.290
So next let's talk
about how we've

00:55:45.290 --> 00:55:48.630
made G Suite more extensible.

00:55:48.630 --> 00:55:50.900
So in G Suite we
make it very easy

00:55:50.900 --> 00:55:53.420
to plug in your own internal
workflow or third party

00:55:53.420 --> 00:55:55.070
applications or data.

00:55:55.070 --> 00:55:57.410
We have a set of tools
called App Maker Apps

00:55:57.410 --> 00:56:01.640
Script and a set of APIs that
allow anyone in your company

00:56:01.640 --> 00:56:04.400
to easily create applications
on top of G Suite,

00:56:04.400 --> 00:56:07.916
leveraging G Suite data,
leveraging third party data,

00:56:07.916 --> 00:56:10.290
and making it easy to integrate
workflow into the G Suite

00:56:10.290 --> 00:56:12.020
experience.

00:56:12.020 --> 00:56:14.690
An example of this is the
Gmail add on framework, which

00:56:14.690 --> 00:56:16.940
we announced earlier this year.

00:56:16.940 --> 00:56:18.560
This lets you create
an application

00:56:18.560 --> 00:56:20.420
that can plug into Gmail.

00:56:20.420 --> 00:56:24.110
So you can think of a lot of
situations where, for your m

00:56:24.110 --> 00:56:26.710
you want to make it easy on
their workflow such as a sales

00:56:26.710 --> 00:56:29.060
person having access
to their CRM data

00:56:29.060 --> 00:56:31.580
plugged into G Suite, when
they are sending emails

00:56:31.580 --> 00:56:34.245
to clients and prospects.

00:56:34.245 --> 00:56:35.620
We're going to
continue to invest

00:56:35.620 --> 00:56:37.030
in this to make it very easy.

00:56:37.030 --> 00:56:39.040
