01:14:17.010 --> 01:14:19.470
Of course, that's all great.

01:14:19.470 --> 01:14:22.110
But what about leveraging
your own data sets

01:14:22.110 --> 01:14:24.674
for artificial intelligence?

01:14:24.674 --> 01:14:26.340
To achieve this, we
give you the ability

01:14:26.340 --> 01:14:29.430
to train your own data sets
using Google Cloud's Machine

01:14:29.430 --> 01:14:31.210
Learning Engine.

01:14:31.210 --> 01:14:34.410
Cloud ML Engine helps
you train your model

01:14:34.410 --> 01:14:37.770
using a custom TensorFlow
graph for any ML use

01:14:37.770 --> 01:14:40.890
case using CPUs or GPUs.

01:14:40.890 --> 01:14:44.320
You can easily train your
model using TensorFlow focusing

01:14:44.320 --> 01:14:45.990
on the creativity
of your solution,

01:14:45.990 --> 01:14:48.877
and leave the
infrastructure to us.

01:14:48.877 --> 01:14:50.960
And when you're done, you
can publish those models

01:14:50.960 --> 01:14:53.510
to make predictions
for your application,

01:14:53.510 --> 01:14:58.520
using simple API calls to
put that training to use.

01:14:58.520 --> 01:15:00.860
So TensorFlow is an
important part of this.

01:15:00.860 --> 01:15:03.020
So what is TensorFlow?

01:15:03.020 --> 01:15:05.390
TensorFlow is an open source
machine learning library

01:15:05.390 --> 01:15:07.105
originally developed by Google.

01:15:07.105 --> 01:15:08.870
And it's used to
create and train

01:15:08.870 --> 01:15:11.030
your machine learning models.

01:15:11.030 --> 01:15:13.970
And the uptake of TensorFlow
has been phenomenal.

01:15:13.970 --> 01:15:16.280
TensorFlow is the most
popular open source machine

01:15:16.280 --> 01:15:19.460
learning project on
GitHub, with contributions

01:15:19.460 --> 01:15:23.440
coming from many
companies, not just Google.

01:15:23.440 --> 01:15:26.110
And in addition to being
a powerful framework,

01:15:26.110 --> 01:15:29.110
TensorFlow was designed
to run anywhere.

01:15:29.110 --> 01:15:31.370
You can run TensorFlow
on our Cloud ML Engine,

01:15:31.370 --> 01:15:33.730
as I just described,
or you can run them

01:15:33.730 --> 01:15:35.950
on your own servers in
your own data centers,

01:15:35.950 --> 01:15:40.980
or anywhere you want,
like mobile devices.

01:15:40.980 --> 01:15:44.220
Now in order to
accelerate your business,

01:15:44.220 --> 01:15:47.880
this typically means building
applications and continuously

01:15:47.880 --> 01:15:52.050
improving them to take advantage
of your data, the insights

01:15:52.050 --> 01:15:56.070
you've derived, as well as
your machine learning models.

01:15:56.070 --> 01:15:59.190
This means creating software
to make important long term

01:15:59.190 --> 01:16:03.960
decisions, as well as real
time decisions like what

01:16:03.960 --> 01:16:05.580
offer to put in
front of a customer

01:16:05.580 --> 01:16:09.030
before they potentially
leave your website.

01:16:09.030 --> 01:16:12.120
Now for your
applications, you want

01:16:12.120 --> 01:16:14.250
to focus on creating
great software,

01:16:14.250 --> 01:16:17.400
not managing infrastructure.

01:16:17.400 --> 01:16:20.120
And the key to being able
to create great software

01:16:20.120 --> 01:16:23.390
is to unleash your
developers from the burdens

01:16:23.390 --> 01:16:26.290
of infrastructure.

01:16:26.290 --> 01:16:28.090
Today, way too
much developer time

01:16:28.090 --> 01:16:31.410
is spent on configuring and
managing hardware and software

01:16:31.410 --> 01:16:32.850
infrastructure.

01:16:32.850 --> 01:16:35.850
This results in your
developers spending their time

01:16:35.850 --> 01:16:40.430
as operators and not
being developers.

01:16:40.430 --> 01:16:42.920
So at Google, our
goal is to allow

01:16:42.920 --> 01:16:46.910
you to accelerate your software
management by making it easy

01:16:46.910 --> 01:16:49.730
for you to build your
applications at global scale

01:16:49.730 --> 01:16:52.730
without needing to know about
or understand the infrastructure

01:16:52.730 --> 01:16:54.420
details.

01:16:54.420 --> 01:16:56.910
Because at the end of the
day, you want your developers

01:16:56.910 --> 01:16:59.850
to be writing code not
patching, operating,

01:16:59.850 --> 01:17:02.070
and scaling infrastructure.

01:17:02.070 --> 01:17:05.820
And that's what your
developers want too.

01:17:05.820 --> 01:17:08.220
So in Google Cloud
we enable this

01:17:08.220 --> 01:17:10.740
by offering you server
lists and container

01:17:10.740 --> 01:17:13.290
solutions at scale
with different options

01:17:13.290 --> 01:17:15.360
to meet your different needs.

01:17:15.360 --> 01:17:17.460
This is provided by the
three services shown here,

01:17:17.460 --> 01:17:20.814
Container Engine, App
Engine, and Cloud Functions.

01:17:23.550 --> 01:17:26.090
Container Engine is our
fully managed Kubernetes

01:17:26.090 --> 01:17:27.510
as a service.

01:17:27.510 --> 01:17:30.550
It's the fastest way to take
advantage of Kubernetes.

01:17:30.550 --> 01:17:32.760
And lets you scale
docker containers easily

01:17:32.760 --> 01:17:34.920
in production.

01:17:34.920 --> 01:17:37.320
And since Google
started Kubernetes,

01:17:37.320 --> 01:17:40.510
it's no accident that GCP
provides the easiest way

01:17:40.510 --> 01:17:43.840
to run Kubernetes in the cloud.

01:17:43.840 --> 01:17:46.860
Container Engine uses the same
open source Kubernetes code

01:17:46.860 --> 01:17:49.260
base to orchestrate
your application,

01:17:49.260 --> 01:17:52.350
allowing you to easily
leverage the rest of GCP

01:17:52.350 --> 01:17:55.090
for running your service.

01:17:55.090 --> 01:17:58.460
And Container Engine handles
all the infrastructure details.

01:17:58.460 --> 01:18:01.730
It schedules and manages your
containers automatically,

01:18:01.730 --> 01:18:05.390
letting you focus
on your application.

01:18:05.390 --> 01:18:08.590
And as a proof point, most of
you will probably remember,

01:18:08.590 --> 01:18:11.860
Niantic used Container
Engine to launch

01:18:11.860 --> 01:18:14.710
their blockbuster
Pokemon Go game.

01:18:14.710 --> 01:18:18.100
That game famously scaled
to many millions of users

01:18:18.100 --> 01:18:22.556
across dozens of countries
over a period of days.

01:18:22.556 --> 01:18:27.340
And they reached a capacity
in scale that was 50 times--

01:18:27.340 --> 01:18:30.730
yes, 50 times their
pre-launch estimates.

01:18:30.730 --> 01:18:32.840
So it was quite an amazing
launch and product.

01:18:36.480 --> 01:18:38.850
Now some application
developers don't even

01:18:38.850 --> 01:18:41.010
want to think about clusters.

01:18:41.010 --> 01:18:44.070
They want to upload their
focus even a notch higher

01:18:44.070 --> 01:18:47.960
to what the industry
is calling server-less.

01:18:47.960 --> 01:18:50.940
Server-less means that there
is no cluster, node nodes,

01:18:50.940 --> 01:18:53.310
no pods to think about.

01:18:53.310 --> 01:18:56.040
All you see when you
implement, deploy, and manage

01:18:56.040 --> 01:19:00.840
your application are
application level constructs.

01:19:00.840 --> 01:19:03.000
In addition, another
benefit of server-less

01:19:03.000 --> 01:19:07.530
is that when your application
consumes little or no resources

01:19:07.530 --> 01:19:11.100
when there's zero load, it can
then quickly start up and scale

01:19:11.100 --> 01:19:14.000
out as load demands it.

01:19:14.000 --> 01:19:18.390
And Google Cloud's App
Engine pioneered server-less,

01:19:18.390 --> 01:19:20.840
empowering applications to
scale from serving one request

01:19:20.840 --> 01:19:23.190
today to quickly
scaling millions

01:19:23.190 --> 01:19:25.870
of requests per second.

01:19:25.870 --> 01:19:27.370
And App Engine
manages everything

01:19:27.370 --> 01:19:28.930
on behalf shown here.

01:19:28.930 --> 01:19:31.690
You just add your
application code.

01:19:31.690 --> 01:19:35.530
And this is really liberated
the developers and big companies

01:19:35.530 --> 01:19:38.110
like Snapchat, Home
Depot, Philips Lightning,

01:19:38.110 --> 01:19:40.960
and other companies, allowing
them to achieve incredible time

01:19:40.960 --> 01:19:43.438
to scale, market, and agility.

01:19:46.120 --> 01:19:48.485
Now if you actually just
want to run some functions

01:19:48.485 --> 01:19:50.110
in the cloud, we have
you covered there

01:19:50.110 --> 01:19:52.270
with Cloud Functions.

01:19:52.270 --> 01:19:54.070
With Cloud Functions,
a developer

01:19:54.070 --> 01:19:57.370
can connect existing
cloud services,

01:19:57.370 --> 01:20:01.794
or build brand new ones in
an entirely server-less way.

01:20:01.794 --> 01:20:03.210
With Cloud Functions,
you only pay

01:20:03.210 --> 01:20:06.290
for the resources consumed while
your functions are running.

01:20:06.290 --> 01:20:09.931
Billion occurs at the
nearest 1/10 of a second.

01:20:09.931 --> 01:20:11.430
And Cloud Functions
can be triggered

01:20:11.430 --> 01:20:16.589
by a growing list of events
generated by GCP services.

01:20:16.589 --> 01:20:18.380
Essentially, we've
designed Cloud Functions

01:20:18.380 --> 01:20:24.460
so it's easy to extend GCP
to fit your business needs.

01:20:24.460 --> 01:20:26.730
In addition, you can use
Cloud Functions from Firebase

01:20:26.730 --> 01:20:27.940
as well.

01:20:27.940 --> 01:20:30.360
This makes it very easy
for mobile developers

01:20:30.360 --> 01:20:33.330
to add server side
extensions without having

01:20:33.330 --> 01:20:37.210
to learn about, configure,
or deal with servers.

01:20:37.210 --> 01:20:39.950
And there are many use
cases for Cloud Functions,

01:20:39.950 --> 01:20:42.890
from the triggering
of examination

01:20:42.890 --> 01:20:46.580
of files that have been recently
uploaded to your cloud storage,

01:20:46.580 --> 01:20:49.270
to triggering events
that are in your logs,

01:20:49.270 --> 01:20:52.400
to reacting to
actions your users are

01:20:52.400 --> 01:20:54.470
taking in your applications.

01:20:57.280 --> 01:21:01.050
And with that, I'm now
pleased to introduce Thomas

01:21:01.050 --> 01:21:04.830
to talk about how Dailymotion
is using Google Cloud to improve

01:21:04.830 --> 01:21:07.381
and accelerate their services.

01:21:07.381 --> 01:21:07.880
Thomas.

01:21:07.880 --> 01:21:09.608
[APPLAUSE]

01:21:09.608 --> 01:21:13.040
THOMAS ACHACHE: Thank
you very much, Brad.

01:21:13.040 --> 01:21:15.090
Thank you very much.

01:21:15.090 --> 01:21:15.960
Hello, everyone.

01:21:15.960 --> 01:21:17.200
I am Tomas Achache.

01:21:17.200 --> 01:21:20.780
I'm the vice president
of DADA at Dailymotion.

01:21:20.780 --> 01:21:23.780
Today we're going to be speaking
of Dailymotion, of course,

01:21:23.780 --> 01:21:27.120
and also about the ways that
we use Google Cloud to power

01:21:27.120 --> 01:21:30.930
our applications and what
difference it makes to us.

01:21:30.930 --> 01:21:34.080
So Dailymotion is one of
the leading video platform

01:21:34.080 --> 01:21:35.220
in the world.

01:21:35.220 --> 01:21:41.070
We have three billion
views and 300 million users

01:21:41.070 --> 01:21:47.250
per month on a catalog
of 150 million videos.

01:21:47.250 --> 01:21:48.900
We have offices in
five continents,

01:21:48.900 --> 01:21:52.680
and we are translating
in 20 languages.

01:21:52.680 --> 01:22:00.720
In terms of data, we receive
3.5 billion events per day.

01:22:00.720 --> 01:22:02.820
But more importantly,
recently, we

01:22:02.820 --> 01:22:06.060
have been through a complete
overhaul of our customer

01:22:06.060 --> 01:22:07.800
experience.

01:22:07.800 --> 01:22:12.180
We have redesigned everything
from bottom to floor,

01:22:12.180 --> 01:22:16.080
and we have relied heavily on
machine learning to do that.

01:22:16.080 --> 01:22:19.800
We have of course, developed
classical algorithms

01:22:19.800 --> 01:22:23.370
for trending videos,
popular channels,

01:22:23.370 --> 01:22:26.940
but we have also done
more special things

01:22:26.940 --> 01:22:31.680
like semantic
notation, which means

01:22:31.680 --> 01:22:36.090
that we try not only to
understand what objects

01:22:36.090 --> 01:22:38.070
were recognized in the video.

01:22:38.070 --> 01:22:41.310
But to understand what
the topic of the video

01:22:41.310 --> 01:22:45.730
was about, on ontology
of 25 million topics.

01:22:45.730 --> 01:22:49.200
So for instance, you
can see any video

01:22:49.200 --> 01:22:53.894
that we have on bitcoin
or [INAUDIBLE] recently.

01:22:53.894 --> 01:22:55.560
This is very important
to us, because it

01:22:55.560 --> 01:22:59.550
helps us to detect what are the
trending topics at the moment.

01:22:59.550 --> 01:23:03.060
This helps our users to have
a topic-based navigation

01:23:03.060 --> 01:23:05.830
to see more videos
on the same topic.

01:23:05.830 --> 01:23:09.360
And it also helps us to
understand better what

01:23:09.360 --> 01:23:11.530
our users are interested about.

01:23:14.470 --> 01:23:20.470
We also host a music
content for universal music.

01:23:20.470 --> 01:23:24.160
And so we have also put
in place neural nets

01:23:24.160 --> 01:23:29.740
to analyze the music
track of our music videos

01:23:29.740 --> 01:23:33.460
so that they can use them in the
representation, a [INAUDIBLE]

01:23:33.460 --> 01:23:37.300
representation of
what they contain.

01:23:37.300 --> 01:23:41.290
And that helps us to
build smart playlists

01:23:41.290 --> 01:23:46.360
to help our users discover
more interesting content

01:23:46.360 --> 01:23:50.150
that is related to the current
clips they're watching.

01:23:50.150 --> 01:23:54.230
Now to come back on GCP
and how we use it, for us

01:23:54.230 --> 01:23:59.550
GCP is really helping us
to increase our speed.

01:23:59.550 --> 01:24:03.440
Obviously, our speed
of development,

01:24:03.440 --> 01:24:07.070
but also it helps us
building better products

01:24:07.070 --> 01:24:10.940
because it allows
us to have our data

01:24:10.940 --> 01:24:14.680
available to our
algorithms faster.

01:24:14.680 --> 01:24:21.270
So on the first point,
on development time,

01:24:21.270 --> 01:24:25.740
so our apps send 3.5
billion events a day.

01:24:25.740 --> 01:24:28.080
And we have developed
a pipeline as it

01:24:28.080 --> 01:24:34.770
goes through Pub/Sub DataFlow,
and BigQuery in real time.

01:24:34.770 --> 01:24:40.290
In terms of development speed,
this took us only two weeks

01:24:40.290 --> 01:24:41.670
to implement.

01:24:41.670 --> 01:24:45.660
Because we could find on
GCP all the basic bricks

01:24:45.660 --> 01:24:50.070
that allowed us to set it up.

01:24:50.070 --> 01:24:52.700
But beyond the speed at
which we have developed

01:24:52.700 --> 01:24:56.670
this, what matters even
more is that now whenever

01:24:56.670 --> 01:24:59.040
we are thinking
about a new product,

01:24:59.040 --> 01:25:01.110
instead of trying
to think about OK,

01:25:01.110 --> 01:25:03.060
what architecture
we're going to use?

01:25:03.060 --> 01:25:03.720
What stack?

01:25:03.720 --> 01:25:05.740
What technologies are
we going to set up?

01:25:05.740 --> 01:25:07.920
How are we going
to maintain them?

01:25:07.920 --> 01:25:13.240
The first question we ask
is can we do this with SQL?

01:25:13.240 --> 01:25:17.500
Because BigQuery
is so fast that it

01:25:17.500 --> 01:25:19.420
can leverage all
the amount of data

01:25:19.420 --> 01:25:22.360
that we send to it in
a matter of seconds.

01:25:22.360 --> 01:25:27.880
And so it's fast enough for most
of our production applications.

01:25:27.880 --> 01:25:29.980
And so with this
pipeline, we are

01:25:29.980 --> 01:25:34.600
able to address issues like
how many concurrent users do

01:25:34.600 --> 01:25:37.570
we have on a given live video?

01:25:37.570 --> 01:25:42.320
We are able to build very
simple recommender algorithms.

01:25:42.320 --> 01:25:45.010
And we also provide
real time analytics

01:25:45.010 --> 01:25:48.070
to the partners [INAUDIBLE]
videos on the platform.

01:25:48.070 --> 01:25:52.180
And it's really helping us
to go faster in our backlog,

01:25:52.180 --> 01:25:58.270
and to release more
features for all our users.

01:25:58.270 --> 01:26:02.000
So having the data
available fast

01:26:02.000 --> 01:26:06.740
can bring marginal improvements
for some algorithms

01:26:06.740 --> 01:26:08.570
like recommendation.

01:26:08.570 --> 01:26:12.620
For some other algorithms,
it is make or break.

01:26:12.620 --> 01:26:16.430
For instance, we have recently
developed a new algorithm

01:26:16.430 --> 01:26:20.040
to detect fraudulent
impressions on the platform.

01:26:20.040 --> 01:26:25.970
And we used to base it on
old Hadoop pipelines that

01:26:25.970 --> 01:26:27.770
took seven hours for
that data to be made

01:26:27.770 --> 01:26:29.660
available to the algorithm.

01:26:29.660 --> 01:26:33.020
When we switched to BigQuery
real time pipelines that I just

01:26:33.020 --> 01:26:37.410
showed, we were able to
intercept 10 times more--

01:26:37.410 --> 01:26:39.650
10 times more
fraudulent impressions,

01:26:39.650 --> 01:26:42.320
because we're more reactive.

01:26:42.320 --> 01:26:45.770
And so to summarize my
message, the accelerations

01:26:45.770 --> 01:26:49.970
that the cloud provides
us is real at Dailymotion.

01:26:49.970 --> 01:26:53.540
It impacts our development
speed and the quality

01:26:53.540 --> 01:26:54.620
of our products.

01:26:54.620 --> 01:26:56.720
And so for us, it
is an investment

01:26:56.720 --> 01:26:58.185
