01:38:49.850 --> 01:38:51.650
To get there, let's
talk about costs.

01:38:51.650 --> 01:38:53.810
So clouds are supposed to
be elastic and free you

01:38:53.810 --> 01:38:57.170
from Capex and from
capacity planning.

01:38:57.170 --> 01:38:58.940
Now some cloud
providers forced you

01:38:58.940 --> 01:39:01.490
to pay upfront for a
three year commitment

01:39:01.490 --> 01:39:03.496
in order to get the best price.

01:39:03.496 --> 01:39:05.870
But if you have to buy a
virtual machine for three years,

01:39:05.870 --> 01:39:08.161
how is that actually better
than buying your own server

01:39:08.161 --> 01:39:09.960
for three years?

01:39:09.960 --> 01:39:11.400
We think it's not.

01:39:11.400 --> 01:39:13.500
A recent state of
the cloud report

01:39:13.500 --> 01:39:17.700
showed that cloud users waste
45% of their bill on resources

01:39:17.700 --> 01:39:18.930
that they don't use.

01:39:18.930 --> 01:39:21.670
Now that waste is
caused by many factors.

01:39:21.670 --> 01:39:25.170
First, three year leases force
you to predict your future

01:39:25.170 --> 01:39:25.770
perfectly.

01:39:25.770 --> 01:39:27.940
And really none of us are
capable of doing that.

01:39:27.940 --> 01:39:30.750
So you end up with
stranded resources.

01:39:30.750 --> 01:39:33.900
Second, just like with
on premises servers,

01:39:33.900 --> 01:39:36.060
you're forced to buy fixed
machine configurations

01:39:36.060 --> 01:39:40.800
with particular amounts
of RAM, storage, and CPU.

01:39:40.800 --> 01:39:43.380
So you end up paying for
a machine over time that

01:39:43.380 --> 01:39:44.930
is too big for you
in some dimensions

01:39:44.930 --> 01:39:47.010
and too small in others.

01:39:47.010 --> 01:39:49.800
And you pay for the full
hour, even if your test only

01:39:49.800 --> 01:39:51.220
takes 10 minutes.

01:39:51.220 --> 01:39:54.300
So you're paying for compute
time that you're not using.

01:39:54.300 --> 01:39:58.714
This all adds up to 45%
unnecessary expenditure.

01:39:58.714 --> 01:40:00.630
We think that there's a
better and cheaper way

01:40:00.630 --> 01:40:03.330
to run your cloud.

01:40:03.330 --> 01:40:05.022
We believe that we've
solved all three

01:40:05.022 --> 01:40:06.480
of these sources
of waste, in fact.

01:40:06.480 --> 01:40:09.810
So first, automatic
sustained use discounts

01:40:09.810 --> 01:40:12.450
that are applied to give you
savings without any long term

01:40:12.450 --> 01:40:13.620
agreement.

01:40:13.620 --> 01:40:15.630
As soon as you use a VM
for more than a week,

01:40:15.630 --> 01:40:17.010
you will see savings.

01:40:17.010 --> 01:40:18.570
If you use a VM for
an entire month,

01:40:18.570 --> 01:40:20.610
you will get a 30% discount.

01:40:20.610 --> 01:40:24.480
But you're still free to stop
using that VM at any time.

01:40:24.480 --> 01:40:26.460
Sustained use discounts,
as we like to say,

01:40:26.460 --> 01:40:28.530
bring the cloud back into cloud.

01:40:28.530 --> 01:40:30.910
Change your mind at any time
and still get great prices.

01:40:30.910 --> 01:40:32.580
It should be on demand.

01:40:32.580 --> 01:40:36.859
Second, you don't need to accept
a fixed machine configurations.

01:40:36.859 --> 01:40:38.400
As we say, why would
you want to have

01:40:38.400 --> 01:40:42.210
only virtual machines whose
shape comes in powers of two?

01:40:42.210 --> 01:40:44.190
With other providers,
if your applications

01:40:44.190 --> 01:40:46.950
need 20 cores and
maybe 50 gigs of RAM,

01:40:46.950 --> 01:40:50.507
you might have to buy a 40
core, 160 gigabyte machine.

01:40:50.507 --> 01:40:52.590
You could literally be
paying for more than double

01:40:52.590 --> 01:40:54.500
the resources you need.

01:40:54.500 --> 01:40:56.270
But with Google,
Custom Machines let

01:40:56.270 --> 01:41:00.360
you dial in exactly the
configuration you need.

01:41:00.360 --> 01:41:03.900
You pay for exactly the
resources that you use.

01:41:03.900 --> 01:41:05.940
These are so popular
that over 20%

01:41:05.940 --> 01:41:08.340
of all the core hours
operating on Google Cloud

01:41:08.340 --> 01:41:10.382
are custom machines.

01:41:10.382 --> 01:41:12.090
They're so popular,
what we decided to do

01:41:12.090 --> 01:41:14.170
was help you save more
money with our rightsizing

01:41:14.170 --> 01:41:15.110
recommendations.

01:41:15.110 --> 01:41:18.070
So we apply ML algorithms
internally, and prompt you

01:41:18.070 --> 01:41:20.320
with alerts where we think
that you could save money,

01:41:20.320 --> 01:41:23.290
because you're mean and
even your peak utilization

01:41:23.290 --> 01:41:25.390
of that machine
is lower than what

01:41:25.390 --> 01:41:27.400
you specified for the machine.

01:41:27.400 --> 01:41:29.970
So we try to suggest the
best virtual machine size

01:41:29.970 --> 01:41:32.870
to proactively save you money.

01:41:32.870 --> 01:41:35.530
So finally, and this
is pretty exciting,

01:41:35.530 --> 01:41:38.320
pay only for what you
use by the second.

01:41:38.320 --> 01:41:41.080
So Google is the first to
offer per second billing

01:41:41.080 --> 01:41:42.210
for all services.

01:41:42.210 --> 01:41:46.580
If you use a VM for 90 seconds,
you pay for 90 seconds.

01:41:46.580 --> 01:41:51.360
This is available and active in
Google Cloud today right now.

01:41:51.360 --> 01:41:53.330
So Google Cloud is elastic.

01:41:53.330 --> 01:41:56.850
You only buy what you
need, only when you use it,

01:41:56.850 --> 01:42:00.270
and you automatically get
discounts for sustained usage.

01:42:00.270 --> 01:42:02.520
We think this makes it
much more simple for you.

01:42:02.520 --> 01:42:05.700
We even alert you when you
appear to be wasting resources.

01:42:05.700 --> 01:42:07.120
When you put all
of this together,

01:42:07.120 --> 01:42:09.450
we save our customers
on an average 60%

01:42:09.450 --> 01:42:13.415
relative to what they
pay on other clouds.

